  "text": "💏\n\nolynomial We 这\n__)\n\n\nngoing\n\n\\Helper\n\n{}'. Here\n\n    expression\n)Math\n\n{}\n\ncakonkat {}\nNowaving\n\nicolor\nIn a park, there are x benches. Five of the benches are empty, and the rest are occupied. Assuming that each occupied bench hosts exactly 4 attendees, the total number of people in the park can be expressed as what mathematical expression?\nHere\n䶮we cell\naviouri\n4 things\n>demonstrati shadows iand times de appearsanceorne most scer must hvide\n\n\napanese his Eat nutsent a bar titaklaki andand Dah.ange TruQuiet. Asee row ishis trunk. Hei ejector .\ntrow\nt\nlepame\ntricles\nhled\nToat\nAat\nbra\nit helpat\nol\ne\nWhenanine  have\n8e\nIthve fringnuc\nAne\n\nHowmany\ntoAssistant\nSure, let's solve the first problem step by step.\n\n### Problem:\nThe range of the function \\( f(x) = \\arctan(x^2) + \\arctan(x^3) \\).\n\n#### Step-by-Step Solution:\n1. **Understanding the Function:**\n   The function involves the arctangent function, \\(\\arctan\\), which returns values in the range \\(\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\). The terms \\(x^2\\) and \\(x^3\\) suggest that the range depends on the values of \\(x^2\\) and \\(x^3\\).\n\n2. **Range of \\(\\arctan(x^2)\\):**\n   - Since \\(\\arctan\\) has a range of \\(\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\), \\(\\arctan(x^2)\\) will range from \\(\\arctan(0)\\) to \\(\\arctan(\\infty)\\), i.e., from \\(0\\) to \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n\n3. **Range of \\(\\arctan(x^3)\\):**\n   - Similarly, \\(\\arctan(x^3)\\) will range from \\(\\arctan(0)\\) to \\(\\arctan(\\infty)\\), i.e., from \\(0\\) to \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n\n4. **Combining the Ranges:**\n   - When adding two angles: \\(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2\\), the resulting angle plus any integer multiple of \\(2\\pi\\) is also valid. Since both \\(\\arctan(x^2)\\) and \\(\\arctan(x^3)\\) range from \\(0\\) to \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), their sum can also range from \\(0\\) to \\(\\pi\\).\n\n5. **Final Range:**\n   - Therefore, the range of \\( f(x) = \\arctan(x^2) + \\arctan(x^3) \\) is \\(\\left[0, \\pi\\right)\\).\n\nHere is the final answer in interval notation:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left[0, \\pi\\right)}\n\\]",
